<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-equivalence-zn-activity">

	<title>Equivalence Relations, Equivalence Classes, and <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m></title>
	<introduction><p><alert>A Strange New World</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Equivalence Relations</title>
		
		<definition>
		<statement><p>Let <m>S</m> be a set and let <m>\sim</m> be a binary relation on <m>S</m>.  Then <m>\sim</m> is called an <term>equivalence relation</term> on <m>S</m> provided that <m>\sim</m> satisfies all of the following properties:
		<ol>
			<li><p><em>Reflexive:</em> For all <m>a \in S</m>, <m>a \sim a</m>.</p></li>
			<li><p><em>Symmetric:</em> For all <m>a, b \in S</m>, if <m>a \sim b</m> then <m>b \sim a</m>.</p></li>
			<li><p><em>Transitive:</em> For all <m>a, b, c \in S</m>, if <m>a \sim b</m> and <m>b \sim c</m> then <m>a \sim c</m>.</p></li>
		</ol>
		</p>
		</statement>
		</definition>
		

<exercise>
	<introduction>
	<p>Which of the properties of reflexive, symmetric, and transitive are satisfied by each of the following relations on the given set <m>S</m>? Sketch a proof or a counterexample to justify each of your answers.</p>
	</introduction>
  
	<task>
      <p>
        <alert>Number of digits relation:</alert> Let
        <m>S = \{1,2,3, \dots, 999, 1000\}</m> and consider the relation
        <m>\sim</m> defined by <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if <m>a</m> has the
        same number of digits as <m>b</m>.
      </p>
    </task>

    <task>
      <p>
        <alert>Divides relation:</alert> Let <m>S = \mathbb{N}</m> and consider
        the relation <m>\sim</m> defined by <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if
        <m>a\mid b</m>.
      </p>
    </task>

    <task>
      <p>
        <alert>Sum to 10 relation:</alert> Let <m>S = \mathbb{N}</m> and
        consider the relation <m>\sim</m> defined by <m>a \sim b</m> if and only
        if <m>a+b = 10</m>.
      </p>
    </task>

    <task>
      <p>
        <alert>Rhyming relation:</alert> Let <m>S</m> be the set of all words in
        the English language and for words <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> say
        <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if <m>a</m> rhymes with <m>b</m>.
      </p>
    </task>

    <task>
      <p>
        <alert>Fraction relation:</alert> Let <m>S = \mathbb{Q}</m> and for
        <m>a,b\in \mathbb{Q}</m> define <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if
        <m>a-b\in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
      </p>
    </task>
</exercise>

