    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Equivalence Classes</title>
		
	<definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\sim</m> be an equivalence relation on a nonempty set <m>S</m>,
        and let <m>a\in S</m>.
        The <em>equivalence class of <m>a</m> </em> (with respect to <m>\sim</m>
        ) denoted <m>[a]_{\sim}</m> is the set of all elements of <m>S</m> that
        are related to <m>a</m> by <m>\sim</m>.
        More precisely,
        <md>
          [a]_{\sim}= \{ x \in S: x\sim a\}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

    <exercise>
    <introduction><p> Let's find some equivalence classes.</p></introduction>
    
    <task workspace="1in">
        <p>
          Find all <em>distinct</em> equivalence classes for the equivalence
          relation defined in 1.
          on the previous page.
          Note equivalence classes are sets so use set notation!
        </p>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p>
          A student claims <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> is an equivalence class for the
          equivalence relation given in 5.
          on the previous page.
          Are they correct?
          Can you find (another) equivalence class for that relation?
        </p>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p>
          In the module prep you hopefully convinced yourself the relation on
          <m>\Z</m> defined by <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if
          <m>a\equiv b \pmod{4}</m> is an equivalence relation.
          Find all distinct equivalence classes of <m>\sim</m>.
          (These are called <em>congruence classes</em>.) How many distinct
          equivalence classes do you think you will get if you replace <m>4</m>
          with <m>n</m>?
        </p>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p>
          We only define equivalence classes for equivalence relations.
          This question is to think about why we might do that.
          What do you get when you find <q>equivalence class</q> for <m>a=3</m> in
          the relation defined in <m>2</m> on the previous page?
          What about <m>a=6</m>?
          How is this different than what happened in previous questions?
        </p>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    
    </paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Some Properties of Equivalence Classes</title>

  	<p>
	The reason we define an equivalence relation to
    have the properties reflexive, symmetric, and transitive is that it leads to
    equivalence classes that have some special properties.
  	</p>

	<exercise>
	<introduction>
        <p>
          We'll start by considering the following lemma.
          <lemma>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Let <m>S</m> be a nonempty set and let <m>\sim</m> be an
                equivalence relation on <m>S</m>.
                Then for all <m>a,b\in S</m>, <m>[a]=[b]</m> if and only if
                <m>a\sim b</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </lemma>
        </p>
       </introduction>

       <task workspace="1in">
              <p>
                Let's see what this lemma means about congruence classes, that
                is, equivalence classes under the equivalence relation
                congruence modulo <m>n</m>.
                Give an example of two elements of <m>Z</m>, say <m>a</m> and
                <m>b</m> for which congruence classes modulo <m>5</m> that are
                equal.
                Is <m>a\sim b</m> where <m>\sim</m> is congruence modulo
                <m>5</m>?
                Then give an example for which <m>a\sim b</m>, is
                <m>[a]_{5} = [b]_{5}</m>?
              </p>

            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
              <p>
                This Lemma means for a given equivalence class there are many
                different ways to write it, we call this <q>picking a
                representative</q>.
                Which representatives might make sense to use for the congruence
                classes modulo <m>8</m>?
              </p>

            </task>
		</exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>
          Now let's consider the following theorem.
          <theorem>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Let <m>S</m> be a nonempty set and let <m>\sim</m> be an
                equivalence relation on <m>S</m>.
                Then <m>S</m> can be written as the disjoint union of distinct
                equivalence classes corresponding to <m>\sim</m>.
                That is,
                <ol>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      For all <m>a,b\in S</m>, if <m>[a] \neq [b]</m> then
                      <m>[a] \cap [b] = \emptyset</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      For all <m>a\in S</m>, <m>a\in [a]</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      For all <m>a\in S</m>, if <m>a\in [b]</m> for some
                      <m>b \in S</m>, then <m>[a] = [b]</m>.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ol>
              </p>
            </statement>
          </theorem>
        </p>

        <p>
          Caption this picture.
          In particular, can you see see parts a,b, and c of the theorem in the
          picture?
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 60%"><image source="images/Equivalence-Z3.png" />
        <p></p>
        </sidebyside>
        
	</exercise>
	
	
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>The Number System <m>\Z_{n}</m></title>

  <p>
    For our purposes, the most important equivalence relation will be congruence
    modulo <m>m</m>.
    This allows us to define the number system <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>:</p>
    <definition>
    <statement><p>For every integer <m>n\ge 2</m>, the
    	<term>integers modulo <m>n</m></term> denoted
    <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>, is the set of the distinct congruence classes of
    <m>\Z</m> modulo <m>n</m>, i.e.,
    <md>
      \Z_{n} = \{[0]_{n}, [1]_{n}, [2]_{n}, \dots, [n-1]_{n}\}.
    </md>
    </p></statement>
    </definition>

  <p>
    We can make <m>\Z_{n}</m> into a number system by defining addition and
    multiplication on the set:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Addition: <m>[a]_{n} + [b]_{n}= [a+b]_{n}</m>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Multiplication: <m>[a]_{n}\cdot [b]_{n} = [a\cdot b]_{n}</m>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    This may seem like a silly thing to do, but remember that <m>[a]_{n}</m> is
    actually an infinite set!
    In fact, the two <m>+</m>'s actually, in a sense, have a different meaning.
    The one on the left is saying <q>add these infinite sets</q> and the one on the
    right is saying <q>add these two integers</q>.
  </p>
  
  <example><p>Let's consider
  <m>
    \Z_{4} = \{ [0]_{4}, [1]_{4}, [2]_{4}, [3]_{4}\}
  </m>
  . And let's find
  <m>
    [3]_{4} + [2]_{4}
  </m>
  . We are really finding:
  <md>
    [3]_{4} + [2]_{4} = \{ \dots, -1, 3, 7, \dots\} + \{ \dots, -2, 2, 6, \dots\}
  </md>
  which is kind of a crazy thing to think about. Using the definition of addition above this is
  <md>
    [3]_{4} + [2]_{4} = [3+2]_{4} = [5]_{4} .
  </md>
  But
  <m>
    [5]_{4}
  </m>
  appears to not be in
  <m>
    \Z_{4}
  </m>
  . Is
  <m>
    \Z_{n}
  </m>
  not closed? Turns out no. Recall that
  <m>
    [5]_{4} = [1]_{4}
  </m>
  . They are the same sets! So we'll say
  <md>
    [3]_{4} + [2]_{4} = [3+2]_{4} = [5]_{4} = [1]_{4} .
  </md>
  In our addition and multiplication tables, we'll write this <q>reduced</q> answer. That is, if we get a congruence class that appears to not be in the set, we will choose a <q>representative</q> that is between
  <m>
    0
  </m>
  and
  <m>
    n-1
  </m>
  .
  </p>
  </example>
  
  <exercise>
	  <p>Complete the following addition and multiplication tables for
          <m>\mathbb{Z}_{4}</m>.
          (We can skip writing the subscripts, as long as you remember it's
          modulo <m>4</m>!)</p>
