<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 3</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Fractions and relations</title>
		<introduction><p>We can define a relation on <m>\mathbb{Q}=\left\{\frac{a}{b}: a,b\in \mathbb{R}, b\neq 0\right\}</m> such that <m>\frac{a}{b} \sim \frac{c}{d}</m> if and only if <m>ad=bc</m>. 

		</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Give 3 examples of pairs that are related.</p></task>
		<task><p>Is this an equivalence relation? Prove or disprove for each of reflexive, symmetric,  and transitive.</p></task>
		<task><p>Find <m>\left[ \frac{1}{2}\right]_\sim</m>.</p></task>
    	</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>When is <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m> a field?</title>
		<statement><p>
			Based on your work for this module, do you have a conjecture for when <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m> is a field? State your conjecture as <q><m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m> is a field if and only if <m>n</m>...</q>. Then, build addition and multiplication tables for two more values of <m>n</m> to test out your conjecture.
		</p></statement>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Multiplicative cancellation</title>
		<introduction><p>One of the things our high school teachers told us about is <q>multiplicative cancellation</q> (although they probably didn't use those words). As an example, if we have <m>6x = 5x^2</m> we could <q>cancel</q> the <m>x</m>'s so that we have <m>6 = 5x</m>. But in high school we were almost always working in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. This question asks about multiplicative cancellation work in <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m>.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>The statement at the start isn't quite precise enough. There's one <m>x</m> value for which the multiplicative cancellation doesn't work, can you find it?</p></task>
		<task><p>Consider <m>n = 12</m>. For <m>[a],[b],[c]\in\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m> with <m>[a] \neq [0]</m>, if <m>[a][c] = [b][c]</m> does <m>[a]=[b]</m>? (Hint: Look at your multiplication table for <m>\mathbb{Z}_{12}</m>!)</p></task>
		<task><p>Consider <m>n = 11</m>.  For <m>[a],[b],[c]\in\mathbb{Z}_{11}</m> with <m>[a] \neq [0]</m>, if <m>[a][c] = [b][c]</m> does <m>[a]=[b]</m>?</p></task>
		<task><p>You should have gotten two different answers for (a) and (b). What's different about 11 vs. 12?</p></task>
		<task><p>What if I told you <m>\mathbb{Z}_{11}</m> is a field? What axioms might you use to prove part (c) is true?</p></task>
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	
